Will this solve the problem?
```
ngOnDestroy() {
  if (this._viewRef) {
     this._viewContainerRef.remove(this._viewContainerRef.indexOf(this._viewRef));
  }
}
```
Without `ngOnDestroy`
![2](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/4223314/24459281/318b8d02-14ac-11e7-9ebf-c488a4edbb06.png)

After applying the code above
![1](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/4223314/24459327/4d838014-14ac-11e7-822f-2a0f431de196.png)

https://plnkr.co/edit/ciBnuI1m6xYO9cyjj5zH?p=preview

@alexzuza it helps indeed :) Your version of ngTemplateOutlet does not leak memory:

![image](https://cloud.githubusercontent.com/assets/247917/24460997/8e7ece10-14a8-11e7-98fa-7b0637fef77d.png)

@ericmartinezr maybe the references are kept by the template outlet and selecting them with @ContentChildren is not necessary for the leak to occur.
